\ core::cmp -- the typed comparisons over a nominal `Ord` trait.
\
\ `Ord` is an ordinary library trait: `cmp ( 'T 'T -- Ordering )`, satisfied
\ nominally by one `impl:` block per numeric width. The six surface
\ comparisons (`eq`/`lt`/`gt`/`lte`/`gte`/`ne`) are derived from `cmp` and its
\ three-variant result, so the compiler itself needs no reserved notion of
\ "ordered" at all -- a user type opts in with its own `impl: Ord`, exactly as
\ any other trait.
\
\ The comparisons are deliberately **not** `inline` here (P7.S3s R5): an
\ `inline` word may declare no `Bound::User` variable at all
\ (`reject_user_bound_on_combinator`), and `Ord` is now one. Each comparison
\ is therefore a real call with a frame, a measured ~2x tax over the old
\ branch-and-construct body on a comparison-heavy loop, accepted for this
\ slice (P7.S3o names the follow-on that could restore the splice).
import: intrinsics i | ueq ult ugt ulte ugte une branch drop swap | ;
import: self::bool b | Bool False True | ;

export: Ord Ordering Less Equal Greater eq lt gt lte gte ne ;

type: Ordering
| Less
| Equal
| Greater
;

trait: Ord 'T
  cmp ( 'T 'T -- Ordering )
;

\ The eight fixed-width integers (`INT_TYPES`, `src/ast.rs`) and
\ `usize`/`isize`: `ult`/`ugt` derive signed/unsigned/float behaviour from
\ the operand type at lowering, so one two-comparison body covers every
\ width -- `a < b` is `Less`, `a > b` is `Greater`, otherwise `Equal`. `branch`
\ reads the raw 32-bit flag `ult`/`ugt` themselves produce, the same
\ primitive `core::bool`'s `if` is built over, so no comparison here goes
\ through the `Bool` it has not yet built.
impl: Ord for i8
  : cmp
    | lhs rhs |
    lhs rhs ult [ Less ] [ lhs rhs ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for i16
  : cmp
    | lhs rhs |
    lhs rhs ult [ Less ] [ lhs rhs ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for i32
  : cmp
    | lhs rhs |
    lhs rhs ult [ Less ] [ lhs rhs ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for i64
  : cmp
    | lhs rhs |
    lhs rhs ult [ Less ] [ lhs rhs ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for u8
  : cmp
    | lhs rhs |
    lhs rhs ult [ Less ] [ lhs rhs ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for u16
  : cmp
    | lhs rhs |
    lhs rhs ult [ Less ] [ lhs rhs ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for u32
  : cmp
    | lhs rhs |
    lhs rhs ult [ Less ] [ lhs rhs ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for u64
  : cmp
    | lhs rhs |
    lhs rhs ult [ Less ] [ lhs rhs ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for usize
  : cmp
    | lhs rhs |
    lhs rhs ult [ Less ] [ lhs rhs ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for isize
  : cmp
    | lhs rhs |
    lhs rhs ult [ Less ] [ lhs rhs ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

\ Both floats. A NaN operand is the one case `Ordering`'s three variants
\ cannot represent: IEEE-754 makes every ordered comparison false, including
\ `x = x`, so a partial order needs a fourth "unordered" answer this enum has
\ no arm for -- mathematically, no single choice of `Less`/`Equal`/`Greater`
\ for a NaN pair can make both `lte` (`Less or Equal`) and `gte` (`Equal or
\ Greater`) come out `False`, since together they cover the whole enum. A
\ real equality check (`ueq`) comes first so a genuinely equal pair still
\ answers `Equal`; only a NaN pair (neither `ueq`, `ult`, nor `ugt` holds)
\ falls to the `Greater` default, which keeps `eq`/`ne`/`lt`/`lte`
\ IEEE-correct for NaN -- in particular `eq` stays the load-bearing one (D4,
\ phase0: NaN is detected via `x = x`). `gt`/`gte` are the residual: they
\ answer `True` for a NaN pair where full IEEE-754 says `False`. A total
\ `Ord` over a partial order is the known, ruled cost of satisfying the
\ numeric tower nominally (P7.S3s R5); a `PartialOrd`/`Ordering?`-shaped
\ split that could recover `gt`/`gte` too is the follow-on, not this slice.
impl: Ord for f32
  : cmp
    | lhs rhs |
    lhs rhs ueq [ Equal ] [ lhs rhs ult [ Less ] [ lhs rhs ugt [ Greater ] [ Greater ] branch ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for f64
  : cmp
    | lhs rhs |
    lhs rhs ueq [ Equal ] [ lhs rhs ult [ Less ] [ lhs rhs ugt [ Greater ] [ Greater ] branch ] branch ] branch ;
;

: eq  ( 'T: Copy Ord 'T -- Bool )
  cmp
  ~[ ( Less )    drop False ]
  ~[ ( Equal )   drop True ]
  ~[ ( Greater ) drop False ]
  Ordering? ;

: lt  ( 'T: Copy Ord 'T -- Bool )
  cmp
  ~[ ( Less )    drop True ]
  ~[ ( Equal )   drop False ]
  ~[ ( Greater ) drop False ]
  Ordering? ;

\ `gt`/`gte` compare with the operands swapped (`a > b` iff `b < a`, `a >= b`
\ iff `b <= a`, true for every value including a NaN pair under IEEE-754,
\ where every ordered relation is `False`) rather than reading `cmp(a,b)`'s
\ `Greater`/`Greater-or-Equal` arms directly. This is what keeps `gt`/`gte`
\ IEEE-correct for a NaN operand: `lib/cmp.sth`'s float `impl: Ord` answers
\ `Greater` for a NaN pair (review fix, so `eq`/`ne`/`lt`/`lte` stay correct
\ for NaN, preserving D4), which would otherwise make `gt`/`gte` answer
\ `True` for NaN if read directly off that same `Greater` arm.
: gt  ( 'T: Copy Ord 'T -- Bool )
  swap cmp
  ~[ ( Less )    drop True ]
  ~[ ( Equal )   drop False ]
  ~[ ( Greater ) drop False ]
  Ordering? ;

: lte ( 'T: Copy Ord 'T -- Bool )
  cmp
  ~[ ( Less )    drop True ]
  ~[ ( Equal )   drop True ]
  ~[ ( Greater ) drop False ]
  Ordering? ;

: gte ( 'T: Copy Ord 'T -- Bool )
  swap cmp
  ~[ ( Less )    drop True ]
  ~[ ( Equal )   drop True ]
  ~[ ( Greater ) drop False ]
  Ordering? ;

: ne  ( 'T: Copy Ord 'T -- Bool )
  cmp
  ~[ ( Less )    drop True ]
  ~[ ( Equal )   drop False ]
  ~[ ( Greater ) drop True ]
  Ordering? ;
